        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Oral health is important to effective work in space, and is also crucial because the oral region serves as an entry point for pathogenic agents, acts as a reservoir for infectious microorganisms, and plays a role in cross contamination and disease transmission.  Oral health studies were performed during all Skylab missions.  Three general areas were studied: clinical dentistry, provisions for in-flight care, and research dedicated to the identification of potential oral problems in long-duration missions.  
<P>
The emphasis of clinical dentistry was on the prevention of dental disease and included pre- and postflight restorative procedures, measurements of oral health indices, and in-flight provisions for emergency dental care.  
<P>
Inflight care dealt with the risks of an unanticipated problem, which could impair effective work.  This risk was computed at 0.92 % for a 3-man 28-day mission, was based on studies of dental experiences in other isolated environments and on a 3-year study of the astronaut population.  The two most likely problems were a painful tooth due to pulpitis or severe, localized gingival inflammation.  Dental caries was not considered a problem in missions up to 3-month duration, because of the high level of oral health of all crewmen and the frequent dental evaluations they received.  
<P>
Oral research was conducted on Skylab by monitoring the crew to assess the effects of the space flight on the population dynamics of oral microflora, the secretion of specific salivary components, and clinical changes in oral health.  Laboratory intraoral changes can precede clinical manifestations of acute and chronic infectious disease.  Clinically detectable alterations of oral tissue can identify changes caused by local and/or systemic disorders of microbial and nonmicrobial origin.  Changes in saliva composition and volume may also reflect local and systemic disorders.  
<P>
One objective of the Skylab oral health studies concerned metabolic profiles that were established to discern microorganisms associated with oral disease conditions.  In addition, effects of space flight on salivary electrolyte levels were determined.  A practical and reproducible method was needed to definitively identify closely related strains of oral microorganisms associated with dental disease.  High resolution gas liquid chromatographic (GLC) techniques provided a means of effectively establishing metabolic profiles between physiologically and serologically closely related, potentially pathogenic oral microbes.  Emphasis was placed on the definitive characterization of <I>Streptococcus mutans</I> since this microorganism has been directly implicated in the etiology of dental caries and indirectly associated with periodontal disease.  A high prevalence of <I>S. mutans</I> in the Skylab crew provided the interest for discerning the different strains of this organism. 
<P>
Salivary electrolytes were also examined in this study.  Changes in the concentration of such electrolytes may be due to emotional stress, drugs, drug toxicity, metabolic changes, and systemic disease.  If the mineral imbalance revealed by the urinary and fecal elevations in specific electrolytes during the Skylab missions is also evidenced by electrolyte changes in saliva, prolonged spaceflight might have an impact on the buffer systems in saliva and upset a basic homeostatic mechanism in the oral cavity.  Thus, this study had two specific aims:<BR>
1.	Adapt and/or develop analytical chemical procedures to establish metabolic profiles for fingerprinting microorganisms associated with oral disease conditions.<BR>
2.	Assess the effects of the 28, 59, and 84-day Skylab missions on the salivary electrolytes.  
<P>
APPROACH:<br>
All Skylab crewmembers took utmost oral care during the preflight timeframe.  Oral hygiene procedures consisted of brushing the teeth two minutes twice a day and flossing once a day.  Regular topical applications of stannous fluoride were provided to all crewmen up to six months before launch; oral exams and evaluation were performed every six months.  Because of the risks of inflammation to the dental pulp, no dental restorations were provided during the last 90 days prior to flight.  Complete oral Panorex radiographs were made of each crewman prior to his mission.  
<P>
All crewmen were placed on a space food diet about 21 days before flight.  The backup crewmen continued on the space diet until launch and prime crewmen until 18 days after recovery.  
<P>
Prime and backup crews of all Skylab missions received two days of training in pertinent dental procedures to be able to treat the most likely dental problems that might arise.   Training included lectures, demonstrations, and supervised clinical procedures, which were as complex as tooth removal, and were performed on volunteer patients.  Instruments and medications were part of the In-flight Medical Support System-Dental, which also included a manual with line drawings of complete intraoral radiographs of each crewman and integrated, illustrated diagnostic, and treatment procedures.  The crew also had air-to-ground communication access with a dentist and/or surgeon.  
<P>
The eighteen prime and backup crewmembers of the three Skylab missions were monitored for quantitative changes in oral microorganisms, saliva partitions considered potentially important to oral health, and alterations in clinical indices of oral health and preexisting dental disease.  Over 100 saliva samples were analyzed.  All samples were collected before oral hygiene and breakfast.  To produce stimulated saliva, crewmembers chewed sterile paraffin and spat into a sterile cup, measuring and recording the time each took to produce a 5-ml volume.  This time was used to calculate the saliva flow rate.  The samples were grouped into three categories, which were preflight/conventional diet, preflight/space diet and postflight/space diet. 
<P>
High resolution GLC and pyrolysis-GLC procedures were used to obtain distinctive metabolic profiles of closely related genera, species and strains of oral microbes associated with dental caries and periodontal disease, with emphasis on serologically unique strains of <I>S. mutans</I>.  Uninoculated culture media and pure cultures of known strains of the same and different serotypes of cariogenic and noncariogenic <I>S. mutans</I>, <I>Streptococcus sanguis</I>, and <I>Lactobacillus fermenti</I>, along with uncharacterized strains of <I>S. mutans</I> isolated from the crew were analyzed.  
 <P>
Microbiological Assessments: Oral specimens were collected from the crewmembers weekly or semiweekly from three intraoral sites from 31 days preflight to 18 days postflight for Skylab 2, from 51 days preflight to 20 days postflight for Skylab 3, and from 57 days preflight to 17 days postflight for Skylab 4.  All collections took place between 7 and 8 am, before oral hygiene procedures or breakfast.  The specimens included dental plaque, crevicular fluid (exudates absorbed from the gingival sulcus area), and stimulated saliva (all sites associated with the development of dental caries, periodontal disease, and alveolar bone loss).  
<P>
Serial 10-fold dilutions of each specimen were plated onto a variety of bacteriologic media for the enumeration of up to 17 microbial categories including mycoplasma and yeast.  Duplicate platings were incubated at 37ºC either aerobically or anaerobically.  
<P>
In addition to microbial assessments, stimulated saliva was used to determine total protein, secretory Immunoglobulin A, and lysozyme.  
<P>

RESULTS:<br>
All preflight dental conditions were resolved before launch.  The radiographs revealed two asymptomatic, previously unrecognized areas of disease conditions about the apex of the teeth of two crewmen, which were successfully resolved.  During the last nine months prior to the Skylab missions, six crewmen required treatment for dental problems, which were other than routine replacement of restorations and dental prophylaxes.   All dental problems were resolved successfully with no recurrence.  No in-flight dental problems requiring the use of the Inflight Medical Support System-Dental occurred during the Skylab 2, 3, and 4 missions.  Laboratory and clinical parameters related to dental injury were evaluated in order to monitor mission-related effects on oral health.  The most distinctive changes observed concerned microbial and IgA concentrations, and analysis of calculus and gingival inflammation.  Increased counts of specific anaerobic and streptococcal components, primarily of the saliva and dental plaque microflora were observed, and were more diet related than flight related.  An elevation in secretory IgA occurred with a reduction of salivary lysozyme.  Also observed was an incidence of increased increments of dental calculus and gingival inflammation, especially on Skylab 2.  There was less increase in the scores of plaque, calculus, and inflammation in the Skylab 3 mission despite this mission being 59 days in length rather than 28 days.  The lower scores are believed to be due to better oral health status of the prime crew of the Skylab 3 mission.  Thus, the preexisting state of dental health was believed to be responsible for the clinical changes rather than any adverse space-flight effect.  These studies show that the better the preflight oral health status, the lesser the flight-related changes on oral health.  
<P>
The high resolution GLC techniques on oral microbes disclosed pronounced metabolic distinctions between two physiologically related genera of the same bacterial family (<I>Streptococcus</I> and <I>Lactobacillus</I> of the family Lactobacillaceae), and between two closely related oral species of the same genus (<I>S. mutans</I> and <I>S. sanguis</I>).  Differences between and within serologically unique strains of the same species of <I>S. mutans</I> were detected, but were less striking than between inter-species profiles.  Overall, the GLC procedures provided a practical and reproducible means of obtaining metabolic markers for identifying closely related strains of pathogenic oral microbes unattainable by conventional techniques.  
<P>
The major pathogenic microorganisms present in crew specimens the morning of launch of Skylab 2 included <I>Klebsiella pneumoniae</I> from the gargle of one crewmember, and <I>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</I> and <I>Staphylococcus aureus</I> from the gargle of another crewmember.  None of these species created any problems during and after flight, although the <I>S. aureus</I> did spread to the mouth and scalp of the crewmember during the mission.  From the gingival fluid, counts of bacteroides, veillonella, fusobacteria and leptotrichia increased significantly in all crewmembers after the initiation of the space diet at 18 days before launch.  Similar increases were noted for species similar to <I>S. mutans</I> and <I>Neisseria</I> sp.  Counts of <I>S. mutans</I>, which were relatively high in the prime crew at the onset of the study, appeared to increase during the recovery period, but this was not statistically significant.  In stimulated saliva, significant increases occurred in mycoplasma, streptococci and bacteroides.  Enteric bacilli, which were unusually numerous in saliva at the onset, declined during the investigative period.  These enterics included isolations of <I>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</I>, <I>Klebsiella pneumoniae</I>, <I>Enterobacter cloacae</I>, and <I>Achromobacter lwoffi</I>.  Changes in microorganisms in the dental plaque were within the expected range.  However, bacteroides, veillonella and lactobacilli increased significantly after the initiation of the space diet.  The conclusion drawn from Skylab 2 was that extensive microbial sampling of the crew before and after the mission revealed no changes of medical significance.  

The most distinctive changes noted from Skylab 3 assessments of gingival fluid, dental plaque and saliva include elevations in counts of specific anaerobic components of the gingival fluid microflora, increased counts of streptococci, enteric bacilli, and specific anaerobic components of saliva, and increased numbers of streptococci, lactobacilli, and leptotrichia in dental plaque.  Except for the post-flight increases of enteric bacilli, the diet was believed responsible for the oral microbial changes.  Preflight regular diet, versus preflight space diet data were compared, along with postflight space diet data.  Specifically, increases were noted in Fusobacteria, <I>Bacteroides melaninogenicus</I>, and leptotrichia in gingival fluid.  <I>Streptococcus salivarius</I>, enterics, fusobacteria, veillonella, and leptotrichia were elevated in stimulated saliva, and <I>S. mutans</I>, lactobacilli, and leptotrichia were elevated in dental plaque.  The microbial changes observed in Skylab 3, with few exceptions, were similar to those noted previously in the ground-based trial and the Skylab 2 mission.  As in the prior studies, the changes occurred during the preflight period after the incorporation of the space diet.  
<P>
The changes detected in Skylab 4 were very similar to those observed in the ground-based study and the two previous Skylab missions.  Most of the microbial changes were diet-related rather than flight-related.  This was shown by statistical comparisons of cumulative counts from the 18 crewmembers before and after the incorporation of space diets during preflight samplings.  The most distinctive changes noted in Skylab 4 included increased counts of specific anaerobic and streptococcal components, primarily of the saliva and dental plaque microflora.  The most significant finding from Skylab 4 and previous Skylab missions was the decrease of intraoral changes.    
<P>
Most of the chemical composition changes in saliva were minimal.  There was an increase in saliva flow rate that was observed with the space diet.  Investigators hypothesized that this increase could have been caused by flight-associated anxiety since stress can affect salivary flow rate.  The total salivary protein decreased in combined missions, although this finding was downplayed since it was significant in only one mission, the 59-day mission, and that corresponded with the increase in salivary flow rate.  The changes in the saliva electrolytes were a decrease in sodium along with an increase in magnesium concentrations.  The increase in magnesium was significant, but since its concentration was extremely low, it was probably not clinically meaningful.  Investigators suggested that the sodium decrease might have stemmed from higher production of adrenocortical steroids, especially aldosterone, which was elevated in urine over each mission.   The increase in IgA was the most prominent compositional change.  This finding was explained as a possible response to a subclinical infection that could have spread among the crew during preflight activity.  This result is similar to the 56-day land-based, simulated Skylab mission.  No clinical problems were detected despite the rise in IgA.  Lysozyme, which is 0.5% to 1% of the total protein, decreased in the middle of the preflight stage, and continued to decrease throughout the study.  This result was unexpected, and investigators were not able to explain the cause.  No changes were noted throughout the missions in saliva calcium, potassium, chloride, phosphate, albumen or IgG concentrations.  The salivary electrolyte changes did not parallel the increased urinary electrolyte excretion observed during each mission.  Thus, the indication here is that saliva does not play a decisive role in regulating the water and electrolyte balance of the body. 
<P>
The Skylab oral health studies results showed that the better the preflight oral health status, the lesser the flight-related changes on general oral health, such as occurrence of cavities, plaque and gingivitis.  Most of the microbial changes were due to switching from conventional diets to space diets, and all Skylab mission data showed only a small effect of microbial intraoral changes.    Chemical changes in saliva, such as protein and electrolyte levels were also minimal.  Thus, the most significant aspect of the investigations was the relative nonexistence of health hazardous intraoral changes during Skylab missions. <END>
